{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":6663803,"ns":0,"title":"Scientific priority","revisions":[{"revid":1374356167,"parentid":1374354629,"minor":false,"timestamp":"2026-09-11T14:28:29Z","size":7219,"sha1":"2539c01729dc2008e79c53c1baff838fa4c6d736","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|Credit for first discovery}}\nIn [[science]], '''priority''' is the credit given to the individual or group of individuals who first made the discovery or proposed the theory. Fame and honours usually go to the first person or group to publish a new finding, even if several researchers arrived at the same conclusion independently and at the same time. Thus, between two or more independent discoverers, the first to publish is the legitimate winner. Hence, the tradition is often referred to as the '''priority rule''', the procedure of which can be summed up in the phrase \"[[publish or perish]]\", because there are generally no prizes for second place in science academia.<ref>{{cite journal|author=Strevens M|year=2003 |title= The Role of the Priority Rule in Science| journal=The Journal of Philosophy |volume=100 |issue=3 |pages=55–79|doi=10.5840/jphil2003100224 |jstor=3655792}}</ref> In a way, the race to be first inspires risk-taking that can lead to scientific breakthroughs which is beneficial to the society (such as discovery of [[malaria|malaria transmission]], [[DNA]], [[HIV]], etc.). On the other hand, it can create unhealthy competition and incentives to publish low-quality findings (e.g., quantity over quality<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Tiokhin|first1=Leonid|last2=Derex|first2=Maxime|title=Competition for novelty reduces information sampling in a research game - a registered report|url= |journal=Royal Society Open Science|year=2019|volume=6|issue=5|article-number=180934|doi=10.1098/rsos.180934|doi-access=free|pmc=6549967|pmid=31218016|bibcode=2019RSOS....680934T}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Phillips|first1=Nathaniel D.|last2=Hertwig|first2=Ralph|last3=Kareev|first3=Yaakov|last4=Avrahami|first4=Judith|date=2014-10-01|title=Rivals in the dark: How competition influences search in decisions under uncertainty|url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0010027714001164|journal=Cognition|language=en|volume=133|issue=1|pages=104–119|doi=10.1016/j.cognition.2014.06.006|pmid=25010397|issn=0010-0277|hdl=11858/00-001M-0000-0024-E7F9-9|s2cid=41705036|hdl-access=free|url-access=subscription}}</ref> or committing [[scientific misconduct]]), which can lead to an unreliable published literature and harm scientific progress.<ref name=fang>{{cite journal|vauthors=Fang FC, Casadevall A|year=2012 |title= Reforming science: structural reforms | journal=Infect Immun|volume=80 |issue=3 |pages=897–901 |pmid= 22184420| pmc=3294664|doi= 10.1128/IAI.06184-11}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Tiokhin|first1=Leonid|last2=Yan|first2=Minhua|last3=Morgan|first3=Thomas J. H.|date=2021-01-28|title=Competition for priority harms the reliability of science, but reforms can help|journal=Nature Human Behaviour|volume=5|issue=7|language=en|pages=857–867|doi=10.1038/s41562-020-01040-1|pmid=33510392|issn=2397-3374|doi-access=free}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Ryan Hill & Carolyn Stein|title=Race to the bottom: Competition and quality in science|url=https://economics.mit.edu/files/20679|access-date=2021-02-22|archive-date=2021-11-25|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211125145026/https://economics.mit.edu/files/20679|url-status=dead}}</ref>\n\n==Priority disputes==\nPriority becomes a difficult issue in the context of ''priority disputes'', in which scientists question who was the first to propose a given theory, make a discovery, or arrive at a particular understanding. Historians of science are generally skeptical of attempts to settle such disputes retrospectively, arguing that these disputes often lack understanding about the nature of scientific change and tend to involve gross misreadings of historical evidence in order to support claims that someone had been overlooked as the original discoverer. Historian and biologist [[Stephen Jay Gould]] once remarked that “debates about the priority of ideas are usually among the most misdirected in the history of science.”<ref>Gould SJ (1977). ''Ontogeny and Phylogeny''. Cambridge, Mass: Harvard University Press, p. 35.</ref>\n\n[[Richard Feynman]] told [[Freeman Dyson]] that he avoided priority disputes by “Always giv[ing] the bastards more credit than they deserve.” Dyson remarked that he also follows this rule, and that this practice is “remarkably effective for avoiding quarrels and making friends.”<ref>Freeman Dyson, 2011, “The Dramatic Picture of Richard Feynman,” New York Review of Books, July 14, 2011. Reprinted in {{ISBN|9781590178546}}</ref>\n\nThe [[Leibniz–Newton calculus controversy]] was a high-profile priority dispute in the 17th century. The [[discovery of Neptune|discoveries of Neptune]] and [[Controversy over the discovery of Haumea|Haumea]] were subject to priority disputes, as were the discoveries of [[Oxygen]] and [[Transfermium Wars|several other chemical elements]]. Priority disputes for the [[Elisha Gray and Alexander Bell telephone controversy|invention of the telephone]] and the [[invention of radio]] both lead to [[priority right|patent dispute]]s.\n\n==See also==\n* [[List of scientific priority disputes]]\n* [[Multiple discovery]]\n* [[Priority right]] in patent law\n* [[Stigler's law]]\n* [[Binomial nomenclature]], where priority is usually enforced through rules\n\n==References==\n{{Reflist}}\n\n==Further reading ==\n* Barbalet, J., \"Science and Emotions\", pp.&nbsp;132–150 in Barbalet, J.(ed), ''Emotions and Sociology (Sociological Review Monograph)'', Blackwell Publishing, (Oxford), 2002.\n* [[Edwin Boring|Boring, E.G.]], \"Cognitive Dissonance: Its Use in Science\", ''Science'', Vol.145, No.3633, (14 August 1964), pp.&nbsp;680–685.\n* Boring, E.G., \"The Problem of Originality in Science\", ''The American Journal of Psychology'', Vol.39, Nos.1-4, (December 1927), pp.&nbsp;70–90.\n* [[Norwood Russell Hanson|Hanson, N.R.]], ''Patterns of Discovery: An Inquiry into the Conceptual Foundations of Science'', Cambridge University Press, (Cambridge), 1962.\n* [[Robert K. Merton|Merton, R.K.]], \"Priorities in Scientific Discovery: A Chapter in the Sociology of Science\", ''American Sociological Review'', Vol.22, No.6, (December 1957), pp.&nbsp;635–659.\n* [https://archive.org/details/sim_proceedings-of-the-american-philosophical-society_1961-10-13_105_5/page/470/mode/2up Merton, R.K. (1961), \"Singletons and Multiples in Scientific Discovery: A Chapter in the Sociology of Science\", Vol.105, No.5, (13 October 1961), pp.&nbsp;470-486.]\n* Merton, R.K., \"Science and Democratic Social Structures\", pp.&nbsp;604–615 in Merton, R.K., ''Social Theory and Social Structure (1968 Enlarged Edition)'', The Free Press, (New York), 1968 [originally published as \"A Note on Science and Democracy\", ''Journal of Legal and Political Sociology'', Vol.1, Nos.1-2, (1942), pp.&nbsp;115–126].\n* Samelson, F., \"History, Origin Myth and Ideology: \"Discovery\" of Social Psychology\", ''Journal for the Theory of Social Behaviour'', Vol.4, No.2, (October 1974), pp.&nbsp;217–232.\n* Samelson, F., \"Whig and Anti-Whig Histories — And other Curiosities of Social Psychology\", ''Journal of the History of the Behavioral Sciences'', Vol.36, No.4, (Fall 2000), pp.&nbsp;499–506.\n\n{{Science and technology studies}}\n\n[[Category:Scientific method]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-10-30T04:18:05Z","lastrevid":1374356167,"length":7219}]}}